PRIVATE REFLECTION: {The core facts are stable across the round: the probe is a chloroalkane-Cy5 analog that covalently labels only HaloTag-eGFP (neutrophils), leaving SNAPtag-DsRed (macrophages) unlabeled; neutrophils therefore carry both eGFP (488 nm) and the far-red dye (~630 nm), while macrophages carry only DsRed (559 nm). The decisive biological point, cited by several colleagues, is the well-established temporal order of leukocyte recruitment after tail-fin injury: neutrophils arrive first (minutes–hours), macrophages later. Consequently the earliest post-injury signals must come from the two neutrophil channels (1 and 2), making D the answer that directly respects the word “first.”  

Competing interpretations: (a) “first” is treated as loose phrasing and the question is read as “which wavelengths ultimately work?” → G; (b) residual/unwashed dye or autofluorescence could produce early 559 nm signal → some colleagues favor C or G. No concrete evidence for either (a) or (b) was supplied, and the protocol explicitly includes a wash step plus dark incubation to minimize photobleaching/unbound dye.  

My prior stance aligned with D; the round reinforced it via the neutrophil-first argument while exposing the ambiguity in wording as the main source of disagreement. No new factual contradictions emerged. Unresolved: precise filter sets/crosstalk values and exact post-injury imaging time point, both of which could modulate the practical “first” signal but do not change the underlying cell-arrival logic.}

